---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const imageBase = "/images/bakes/2026/bake001";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Bread Pitt's First Big Role"
  description="Bake001: Bread Pitt 2.0's first loaf, a 50/50 white and whole-wheat sourdough baked July 29–30, 2026."
>
  <article class="story-page wrap">
    <a class="back-link" href="/bakes/"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> All bakes</a>

    <header class="story-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Bake001 · July 29–30, 2026</p>
      <h1>Bread Pitt's First Big Role</h1>
      <p class="story-intro">Everybody remembers the first loaf... especially the baker.</p>

      <dl class="meta-list">
        <div>
          <dt>Recipe</dt>
          <dd><a href="/recipes/fifty-fifty-white-whole-wheat-sourdough/">50/50 White & Whole Wheat</a></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Starter</dt>
          <dd><a href="/starter/">Bread Pitt 2.0</a></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Rating</dt>
          <dd>8/10 thumbs up</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Finished temp</dt>
          <dd>209°F</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>

    <figure class="story-image story-image-feature">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-final-2026-07-30.jpg`} alt="Finished Bake001 sourdough loaf cooling on a rack" />
      <figcaption>Bread Pitt's first finished loaf.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <p>Bread Pitt 2.0 had been alive for a few weeks. If you had been following along, congratulations—you were the first.</p>
      <p>Rebooting my Bread Pitt sourdough starter was a bit of a struggle, but he finally made it. You can follow his progress <a href="/starter/">here</a>.</p>
      <p>Version 1.0 didn’t survive the move from Florida to Texas, so this wasn’t my first sourdough loaf ever. I’ve baked quite a few over the years. Some were pretty good. Some... well... let’s just say they still made decent toast.</p>
      <p>I wasn’t expecting the perfect Instagram loaf. I was just trying to get Bread Pitt back in the game.</p>
      <p>I fed him at about 4:40 in the morning, and around 2:00 that afternoon, I decided he was as ready as he was going to get.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-starter-at-start-2026-07-29.jpg`} alt="Bread Pitt starter ready for Bake001" />
      <figcaption>Bread Pitt at the start of Bake001.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>Enter Dough Cloony</h2>
      <p>Meet my KitchenAid mixer, which officially answers to Dough Cloony.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/recipes/fifty-fifty-white-whole-wheat-sourdough/">recipe</a> wasn’t anything fancy—my 50/50 white and whole-wheat sourdough. It has become one of my favorite everyday loaves because it has more flavor than plain white bread without becoming too dense or overly rustic.</p>
      <p>In case you’re wondering what “rustic” means, it is sometimes just a euphemism for “hard to chew.”</p>
      <p>The autolyse went exactly as planned. The flour and water came together, then sat quietly while Bread Pitt finished waking up for his big day.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image-grid">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-mixer-dough-cloony-2026-07-29.jpg`} alt="KitchenAid mixer named Dough Cloony" />
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-autolyze-2026-07-29.jpg`} alt="Dough during autolyse" />
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <p>Once the starter and salt went in, Dough Cloony took over.</p>
      <p>The dough came together fairly quickly, and after a few minutes of mixing, it looked ready for the next stage.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-mixing-in-progress-2026-07-29.jpg`} alt="Bake001 dough mixing in Dough Cloony" />
      <figcaption>Dough Cloony mixing everything together.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>Trust the Process</h2>
      <p>Stretch the dough.</p>
      <p>Fold it over.</p>
      <p>Wait thirty minutes.</p>
      <p>Repeat.</p>

      <h3>First fold</h3>
      <p>The first stretch wasn’t particularly encouraging.</p>
      <p>The dough wanted to tear more than stretch, and I had strands breaking almost immediately.</p>
      <p>That’s completely normal.</p>
      <p>I hope.</p>
      <p>By the end of the first set, though, the dough had already tightened up noticeably.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image-grid">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-start-of-bulk-ferment-2026-07-29.jpg`} alt="Dough before first stretch-and-fold" />
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-bulk-ferment-progress-w-dough-temp-2026-07-29-01.jpg`} alt="Dough after first stretch-and-fold" />
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h3>Second fold</h3>
      <p>Thirty minutes later, things looked different.</p>
      <p>The dough stretched farther before resisting and folded back over itself much more cleanly.</p>
      <p>Instead of fighting me, it was starting to cooperate.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image-grid">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-bulk-ferment-progress-w-dough-temp-2026-07-29-02.jpg`} alt="Dough before second stretch-and-fold" />
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-bulk-ferment-progress-w-dough-temp-2026-07-29-03.jpg`} alt="Dough after second stretch-and-fold" />
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h3>Third fold</h3>
      <p>By the third round, it finally felt like dough instead of a sticky science experiment.</p>
      <p>It stretched nicely before tightening up again, and I could feel the gluten network starting to do its job.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image-grid">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-bulk-ferment-progress-w-dough-temp-2026-07-29-04.jpg`} alt="Dough before third stretch-and-fold" />
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-bulk-ferment-progress-w-dough-temp-2026-07-29-05.jpg`} alt="Dough after third stretch-and-fold" />
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>The Waiting Game</h2>
      <p>With the folds complete, it was time for bulk fermentation.</p>
      <p>The dough settled into its container while Bread Pitt got to work.</p>
      <p>One thing I kept an eye on throughout the evening was the temperature. The dough stayed remarkably consistent at around 76°F, which was almost exactly where I wanted it.</p>
      <p>That was one variable I didn’t have to worry about.</p>
      <p>Waiting, on the other hand...</p>
      <p>Waiting is hard. At least that’s what Tom Petty told me.</p>
      <p>Every sourdough recipe says something like, “Bulk ferment for 10–12 hours.”</p>
      <p>Real life doesn’t always cooperate with recipes.</p>
      <p>I wasn’t planning to stay awake until four in the morning just to watch bread ferment. Instead, I kept checking the dough and watching for signs of growth rather than staring at the clock.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-bulk-ferment-progress-w-dough-temp-2026-07-29-06.jpg`} alt="Bake001 dough during bulk fermentation at about 76 degrees Fahrenheit" />
      <figcaption>Bulk fermentation at about 76°F.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>Real Life Beats Perfect Timing</h2>
      <p>Around 10:00 that night, I had a decision to make.</p>
      <p>Stay awake?</p>
      <p>Wake up in the middle of the night?</p>
      <p>Or admit that I enjoy sleeping?</p>
      <p>The refrigerator won.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image-grid">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-bulk-ferment-progress-w-dough-temp-2026-07-29-08.jpg`} alt="Bake001 dough before refrigeration at about 10 PM" />
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-bulk-ferment-progress-w-dough-temp-2026-07-30-03.jpg`} alt="Bake001 dough after refrigeration and morning fermentation" />
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <p>I tucked the dough into the fridge and picked the project back up around 4:00 the next morning. I hadn’t planned to start again at four, but that was when I woke up. Once I remembered there was dough in the refrigerator, I couldn’t go back to sleep.</p>
      <p>People often think sourdough baking is incredibly rigid, but it is actually pretty forgiving once you understand what the dough is doing.</p>
      <p>I’m not saying I understand it. I’m just getting better at guessing.</p>
      <p>Sometimes your schedule dictates the bread instead of the other way around.</p>
      <p>That’s okay.</p>
      <p>Or at least that’s what I keep telling myself.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>The Great Parchment Experiment</h2>
      <p>Then came an unexpected plot twist.</p>
      <p>I reached for the parchment paper.</p>
      <p>There wasn’t any.</p>
      <p>Why would there be? I hadn’t bought any since moving into the apartment.</p>
      <p>Normally, I bake in my KitchenAid bread bowl with parchment underneath the loaf.</p>
      <p>Not today.</p>
      <p>I lightly greased and floured the baking surface and hoped for the best.</p>
      <p>Would that work?</p>
      <p>Well... I guess we were finding out.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-floured-vessel-no-paper-2026-07-30.jpg`} alt="Floured KitchenAid bread bowl baking surface without parchment paper" />
      <figcaption>No parchment. Time to find out.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>Oops...</h2>
      <p>Now you just put it in the oven and wait.</p>
      <p>Right?</p>
      <p>I forgot to score the loaf.</p>
      <p>It was already sitting in the oven before my brain finally caught up.</p>
      <p>Fortunately, it had only been in there for a minute or two.</p>
      <p>Out it came.</p>
      <p>Quick score.</p>
      <p>Back into the oven.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-back-out-for-score-2026-07-30.jpg`} alt="Bake001 loaf briefly removed from oven for scoring" />
      <figcaption>The score that almost didn't happen.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <p>Not ideal, but certainly not the end of the world.</p>
      <p>Or so I told myself.</p>
      <p>Mistakes almost never ruin bread as dramatically as you think they will. Most of the time, they just teach you something for the next loaf.</p>
      <p>Usually.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>Opening Night</h2>
      <p>Twenty-five minutes under the lid...</p>
      <p>Then the reveal.</p>
      <p>This is the moment that is either exciting or disappointing.</p>
      <p>You spend hours looking at a lump of dough, and suddenly there is an actual loaf sitting in front of you.</p>
      <p>It might still be a lump.</p>
      <p>Or it might actually look like bread.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-after-first-25-min-bake-2026-07-30.jpg`} alt="Bake001 loaf after 25 minutes covered" />
      <figcaption>The first reveal.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <p>Even before the final part of the bake, I could tell this one had good oven spring.</p>
      <p>After another twenty minutes uncovered, the internal temperature reached 209°F.</p>
      <p>Exactly where I wanted it.</p>
      <p>I was actually aiming for 208°F, but let’s allow a little revisionist history.</p>
      <p>The best surprise?</p>
      <p>The loaf lifted cleanly out of the bread bowl without any parchment at all.</p>
      <p>Apparently, I may not have needed it all along.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image story-image-feature">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-final-2026-07-30.jpg`} alt="Finished Bake001 sourdough loaf" />
      <figcaption>The finished loaf.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>The Crumb Shot</h2>
      <p>Every sourdough baker waits for the crumb shot.</p>
      <p>It is the report card.</p>
      <p>The loaf had a great crust, good color, and plenty of oven spring.</p>
      <p>Inside, there were a couple of larger air pockets just beneath the score. That usually means the loaf could have proofed a little longer before baking.</p>
      <p>Not a failure.</p>
      <p>Just feedback.</p>
      <p>The rest of the crumb was exactly what I wanted: fully baked, soft, flavorful, and perfect for sandwiches or toast.</p>
      <p>That is one of the things I enjoy most about sourdough. Every loaf teaches you something.</p>
      <p>Sometimes the lesson is huge.</p>
      <p>Sometimes it is just another twenty minutes of proofing.</p>
      <p>Either way, the next loaf gets a little better.</p>
      <p>Probably.</p>
      <p>Hopefully.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="story-image story-image-feature">
      <img src={`${imageBase}/bake001-crumb-2026-07-30.jpg`} alt="Crumb of Bake001 50/50 white and whole-wheat sourdough loaf" />
      <figcaption>The final crumb.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="story-section">
      <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
      <p>Bread Pitt’s first loaf probably won’t win any Hollywood awards, but I give it eight out of ten thumbs up.</p>
      <p>The crumb could have been a little better, but the bread was delicious.</p>
      <p>More importantly, Bread Pitt is back.</p>
      <p>After losing my original starter during the move to Texas, it would have been easy to buy another one or let the hobby quietly fade away.</p>
      <p>I rarely do things the easy way.</p>
      <p>Honestly, I think that is what this website is really about.</p>
      <p>Not showing perfect bread.</p>
      <p>Showing real bread.</p>
      <p>The good loaves, the bad loaves, the mistakes, the victories, and the process of learning how to adapt when things don’t go according to plan.</p>
      <p>Because that is how you actually get better.</p>
      <p>And besides...</p>
      <p>Even the loaves that don’t turn out exactly the way I planned are still usually pretty good with butter.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
